  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 115 PM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nOn Tuesday a diffused upper level trough makes its way into the Four \nCorners region. This feature would flatten the upper level ridge \nthat has been parked over the area the last few days. The trough \nwould also bring an uptick in moisture, perhaps enough to kick off a \nfew showers and storms over higher elevations. Even with the \nweakened ridge, our flow is still primarily out of the northwest and \nthe surface will remain dry and warm. Afternoon high temperatures \nare likely to be in the low to mid 80s north and east of I-25. Mid \nto high 70s possible west. Relative humidity values in the teens \ncould promote elevated fire weather conditions. Thankfully winds are \nnot expected to be particular strong and overnight recoveries are \nfair.\n\nWednesday through Saturday is a rather uneventful stretch from a \nmeteorological perspective. Afternoon highs stay above average in \nthe high 70s to mid 80s. Mostly clear skies are expected. There are \nno clear signals in model guidance for an appreciable disturbance, \njust weak zonal flow on the north end of a weak upper level ridge. \nWe may have to wait until next weekend for a chance of a disturbance \nin this stagnant pattern.",
